    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **65 tests, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe create-owner pipeline already logged a human-readable audit line in `AuditOwnerCreated`. I added the required immutable structured event alongside it.\n\n**`AuditOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 after the existing audit line, it now emits a second `AUDIT` line: a JSON object `{seq, ownerId, customerCode, membershipLevel, event:\"OWNER_CREATED\"}`.\n- `seq` comes from a `static AtomicLong`, so it increases monotonically across every create process-wide.\n- The map is a `LinkedHashMap`, so the serialized JSON preserves the specified field order.\n- Serialized once with Jackson 3 (`tools.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper`) into an immutable string \u2014 nothing mutates the owner.\n\n**`Owner.java`** \u2014 added `getPrimaryIdentifier()`, a single source of truth that returns `customerCode` today. The event's identifier field reads from this method rather than `getCustomerCode()` directly. That's the key to the forward-compatibility clause: when the customerCode is later unified into the memberId, only `getPrimaryIdentifier()` changes and the `OWNER_CREATED` event automatically carries the memberId instead \u2014 no edit to `AuditOwnerCreated` needed.\n\nThe new `Cp53Tests` (asserting one `AUDIT` event contains `OWNER_CREATED`, the owner id, and the customerCode) passes, and all 64 prior tests still pass.",
